WALK = [(0, None, [('senya', 'пропала'), ' \'Chef, where has our big stove disappeared to?\' They express \'where\' by implies direction, and this word is "vanished", with a she-word ending because They express \'stove\' by is a she-word word. Senya sees an empty space.']), (1, None, [('barinov', 'отвёз'), ' \'I personally took it to a reliable repair service.\' This word is "took away" by vehicle, not on foot. We hear \'reliable\' grows from We hear \'hope\', and the first word means "personally".']), (2, None, [('senya', 'вчера'), ('senya', 'вечером'), ' \'But it was working perfectly yesterday evening!\' This phrase is "yesterday in the evening": When they say \'evening\' takes an instrumental ending. The stove is "she" in Russian, so When they say \'worked\' takes a she-word ending.']), (3, None, [('barinov', 'профилактики'), ' \'I turned it in for important preventive maintenance.\' The second word is "handed in". This word, prophylaxis, is a doctor\'s word Russians also use for servicing machines. It sounds impressive, and that\'s why he uses it.']), (4, None, [('senya', 'на'), ('senya', 'чём'), ' \'And on what are we to cook today?\' Russians cook "on" a stove, so it\'s this phrase, "on what". In Russian, \'are we to cook\' translates literally translates to "to us to cook". Senya is practical.']), (5, None, [('barinov', 'плитке'), ' \'For now cook the dishes on this little hotplate.\' Here, \'for now\' shows is also used for "while" and "bye". This word is the word for stove with a diminutive ending, meaning a little hotplate. The command to cook is addressed to the whole kitchen.']), (6, None, [('senya', 'нагревается'), ' \'This little hotplate heats up much too slowly!\' This The way to express \'heats up\' has a self-directed ending, meaning "heats itself". The following words mean "too" and "slowly". Senya complains, and he has every right.']), (7, None, [('barinov', 'Хватит'), ('barinov', 'ныть'), ' \'Stop whining, cooks must know how to do everything!\' This phrase uses "enough" as common speech for "stop it", followed by the action word "to whine". \'must know how\' is used here uses a action \'to be able\' is used here, indicating a skill.']), (8, None, [('senya', 'заказы'), ' \'The guests will wait for their orders very long!\' Listen to how \'will wait\' is made from "will be" plus the dictionary form. This word is "orders", the multiple form.']), (9, None, [('barinov', 'четвёрку'), ' \'Set the heat to four and get to work!\' The sentence says "put the heating on a four": this word is the number four as a thing word, which is also a school mark. The commands to put and to work are addressed to everyone.']), (10, None, [('senya', 'нашу'), ('senya', 'старую'), ('senya', 'плиту'), ' \'When will they return our old stove to us?\' The action word is "they will return", with no one named. This phrase ends with accusative markers three times, because the stove is being returned. Senya knows there\'s no answer.']), (11, None, [('barinov', 'привезут'), ' \'In a day or two they will bring everything!\' We hear \'in a day\' literally means "through a day". This word is "will bring" by vehicle, similar to We hear \'took away\'. Barinov\'s plan depends on nobody checking.'])]